      "problem": "Let $f(x) = \\sin{x}.$ Calculate the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nP_4(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\dfrac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 + \\dfrac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3 + \\dfrac{f^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^4.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ $f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ $f''\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ $f'''\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $f^{(4)}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & \u27f9 &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2034}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P_4(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
